id: ce-oem-kernel-driver/pci-driver-conflict
category_id: kernel-driver
_summary: Check that no second loaded module matches a bound PCI device
_description:
    Two kernel modules can match one PCI device, for example an in-kernel
    driver and a vendor driver. The kernel loads both, and the first one that
    probes gets the device. A module that probes first and fails can leave
    side effects: on Jetson Orin, rtw88_8822ce takes phy0 before its probe
    fails, so the Wi-Fi of the vendor driver rtl8822ce becomes phy1.
    The job fails when a loaded module matches a PCI device that is bound to
    another module, and the loaded module drives no PCI device. Usually the
    image must block such a module. The job does not report a module that
    uses the bound module (for example nvidia_drm), or a module of a driver
    family that declines the device by design: i915/xe, radeon/amdgpu, and
    the users of snd_intel_dspcfg (for example snd_hda_intel with SOF).
    Usage of parameter (optional, modules separated by spaces or commas):
    PCI_DRIVER_CONFLICT_IGNORE="module_a module_b"
plugin: shell
estimated_duration: 2
environ: PCI_DRIVER_CONFLICT_IGNORE
command:
    pci_driver_conflict.py --ignore "${PCI_DRIVER_CONFLICT_IGNORE:-}"
